<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Estonian Self-Administration</h1><p class="st-meta">1941 CE – 1944 CE · capital Tallinn</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Estonian collaborationist administration under German occupation in 1941-1944, headed by Hjalmar Mäe within Reichskommissariat Ostland.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>German forces drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> out of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Estonia</a> in summer 1941. The occupiers placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> in the Generalkommissariat <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-estonia-1561-1721">Estland</a> within <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/reichskommissariat-ostland">Reichskommissariat Ostland</a>, and in 1941 appointed a Directorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hjalmar-mae">Hjalmar Mäe</a> to handle local administration.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Self-Administration had limited powers under German control and could not restore independence. It ran local government, schools and the economy, and took part in German policies. Almost all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> who had not fled were murdered in 1941, and camps such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klooga-concentration-camp">Klooga</a> held Jews deported from elsewhere. From 1943 Estonian men were conscripted into German units.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> advanced in September 1944 and the Germans withdrew, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hjalmar-mae">Mäe</a> left, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-tief">Otto Tief</a> briefly formed a national government in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/reval">Tallinn</a> on 18 September. Soviet forces took Tallinn on 22 September 1944, restoring the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/estonian-soviet-socialist-republic">Estonian Soviet Socialist Republic</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/estonian-self-administration.jpg" alt="Estonian Self-Administration" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Preisi_riigin%C3%B5unik_Litzmann_ja_Eesti_Omavalitsuse_juht_haridusdirektor_H._M%C3%A4e.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1941 CE – 1944 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tallinn</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hjalmar Mäe</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Estonian, German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/estonia">Estonia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Eesti Omavalitsus, Estonian Directorate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1942&amp;focus=estonian-self-administration" aria-label="Estonian Self-Administration on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5201 181 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Estonian Self-Administration lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5521 437L5520 501"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5520 501h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5521 437h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5521 437h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tallinn. <a href="/atlas?year=1942&amp;focus=estonian-self-administration">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

